# ---------------- product-deep-dive ----------------
pd = {"id": "product-deep-dive", "name": "Product Deep-Dive",
"description": "The stack in depth: Allowlisting, Ringfencing, Storage, Elevation, Network, Web Control, and Cyber Hero MDR \u2014 how they compose into Zero Trust.",
"icon": "\U0001f9f1", "scenarios": []}
pd_lessons = [
L("product-deep-dive", "The stack: six controls, one philosophy",
["Allowlisting: deny-by-default execution \u2014 only approved applications run. The foundation; everything else assumes it.",
"Ringfencing: containment of the allowed \u2014 what approved apps may do, across app-to-app, file, registry, and network dimensions.",
"Storage Control: deny-by-default for removable media \u2014 serial-scoped, user-bound, time-boxed device approval with encryption options.",
"Elevation Control: just-in-time, per-app, time-boxed admin rights \u2014 standing admin replaced, every session logged.",
"Network Control: host firewall under deny-by-default \u2014 egress allowlisted by destination, inbound denied by default.",
"Web Control: deny-by-default for browsing \u2014 category policy, download file-type rules, the delivery vector governed.",
"Cyber Hero MDR: the 24/7/365 US-based human layer \u2014 ~60-second average response. Technology plus people: prevention with a white-glove backstop."],
"STACK MAP\n  execute:   Allowlisting (may it run?)\n  behave:    Ringfencing (what may it do?)\n  media:     Storage Control (may data leave?)\n  privilege: Elevation Control (may it elevate?)\n  network:   Network Control (may it reach?)\n  web:       Web Control (may it fetch?)\n  humans:    Cyber Hero MDR (24/7/365, ~60-sec)",
"Memorize the stack map: seven lines, each answering one question. In interviews, the map lets you place any scenario ('where would this be caught?') in seconds \u2014 that fluency is the product-knowledge signal.",
["Listing products without the questions they answer \u2014 the map is the understanding.",
"Forgetting Cyber Hero \u2014 the human layer is part of the stack.",
"Inventing products or features \u2014 public concepts only, always."],
"Recite the stack map from memory: seven lines, one question each. Time yourself."),
L("product-deep-dive", "How the controls compose",
["The controls are layered, not redundant: Web Control governs the fetch, Allowlisting governs the execution, Ringfencing governs the behavior, Network/Storage govern the exfiltration paths, Elevation governs the privilege. Each catches what the earlier ones missed.",
"The composition story: a phishing document arrives (Web Control: allowed, trusted sender) \u2192 macro tries PowerShell (Ringfencing: denied) \u2192 the deny triggers investigation (Cyber Hero / analyst) \u2192 the delivery gets hunted. The incident is a LAYERED story \u2014 tell it that way.",
"Defense in depth means correlated signals: a ringfence deny PLUS an egress deny, same host, same hour, is an intrusion pattern \u2014 not two policy events. Interviewers test whether you see the composition or just the components.",
"The gaps are honest: no control covers malicious USE of fully-approved apps within their leashes, or the human clicking through. Name the limits when asked \u2014 candor about composition limits is senior."],
"LAYERED STORY\n  fetch:     Web Control allows (trusted sender)\n  execute:   Allowlisting allows (signed Word)\n  behave:    Ringfencing DENIES (Word -> PowerShell)\n  investigate: the deny is a detection\n  hunt:      the delivery, laterally",
"Practice narrating the layered story for three attack shapes: macro document, malicious download, USB drop. The narration \u2014 which layer, what it saw \u2014 is the product-depth performance.",
["Single-layer answers \u2014 'the allowlist stops it' misses the composition.",
"Treating layers as redundant \u2014 each answers a different question.",
"Hiding the limits \u2014 honest gaps read as seniority."],
"Narrate the layered story for a malicious USB drop: which layers engage, in what order?"),
L("product-deep-dive", "Allowlisting depth: the interview version",
["The ninety-second case: detection classifies AFTER execution begins \u2014 fileless, living-off-the-land, and zero-day techniques execute before any verdict exists. Allowlisting asks the question BEFORE execution: is this explicitly trusted? Unknown code never runs; no signature needed.",
"The operational honesty: the cost is real \u2014 every legitimate new app needs an approval path. Learning Mode, certificate rules, the Approval Center, the Testing Environment: the machinery that pays the cost. Interviewers listen for the tradeoff, not just the triumph.",
"Rule strength in one breath: certificate (survives updates) over hash (breaks every version) over path (never in user-writable locations). The version-change false positive is the classic interview probe \u2014 answer with the certificate rule.",
"The EDR relationship: allowlisting doesn't replace detection \u2014 it shrinks what detection must chase. Prevention first, detection as the backstop. Respect the panel's detection engineers; don't declare their field obsolete."],
"90-SECOND CASE\n  detection: classifies AFTER execution starts\n  allowlisting: asks BEFORE: explicitly trusted?\n  cost: real \u2014 paid via Learning Mode, cert rules,\n         Approval Center, Testing Environment\n  EDR: still vital \u2014 smaller, higher-signal workload",
"Rehearse the ninety-second case aloud until it's smooth. It's the answer to 'why allowlisting,' 'why not just EDR,' and 'isn't this too strict' \u2014 one case, three questions.",
["'EDR is obsolete' \u2014 never trash the panel's field.",
"Skipping the operational cost \u2014 the tradeoff is the credibility.",
"Hash-rule answers for churning apps \u2014 the certificate rule is the probe's answer."],
"Deliver the ninety-second case aloud. Then deliver it again, 30 seconds shorter."),
L("product-deep-dive", "Ringfencing depth: the interview version",
["The one-liner: 'Word is allowed to run, but it may not launch PowerShell, read the credential store, or reach the internet \u2014 so a hijacked Word cannot become ransomware.' One image, four dimensions implied, business outcome stated.",
"The four leashes when pressed: application-to-application (may it launch others?), file (what may it touch?), registry (what keys?), network (where may it go?). Name them crisply \u2014 then return to the one-liner.",
"The threat model: living-off-the-land. Attackers don't bring malware; they bring instructions for your trusted tools. Ringfencing assumes the allowed app is already compromised \u2014 that's the Zero Trust sentence.",
"The executive translation is the test: CFOs remember 'Word on a leash,' not 'API-hooking minifilter.' Translate every time, unprompted, when the audience is non-technical."],
"RINGFENCING (three depths)\n  one-liner:  'Word on a leash'\n  four leashes: app-to-app, file, registry, network\n  threat model: living-off-the-land; assume the app\n                is already compromised",
"Practice all three depths back to back: one-liner (10 sec), leashes (30 sec), threat model (30 sec). The interview picks the depth; you have them all ready.",
["Leading with kernel mechanics \u2014 the one-liner first, always.",
"Forgetting the assume-breach framing \u2014 it's the Zero Trust sentence.",
"One depth only \u2014 prepare all three; the panel varies."],
"Deliver the three depths aloud: 10 seconds, 30 seconds, 30 seconds. Then the CFO version."),
]
