# ---------------- zero-trust-philosophy ----------------
zp = {"id": "zero-trust-philosophy", "name": "Zero Trust Philosophy",
"description": "Never trust, always verify: the philosophy beneath the product \u2014 deny-by-default, assume breach, least privilege \u2014 and defending it in interviews.",
"icon": "\U0001f9e0", "scenarios": []}
zp_lessons = [
L("zero-trust-philosophy", "Never trust, always verify",
["Zero Trust is not a product \u2014 it's the design principle: no implicit trust based on network location, identity history, or past approval. Every access, every execution, every connection is verified against policy, every time.",
"The three operational translations: DENY BY DEFAULT (unknown is denied until proven trusted), ASSUME BREACH (design as if the attacker is already inside \u2014 leashes, not just walls), LEAST PRIVILEGE (every grant is the minimum sufficient, bounded in scope and time).",
"Applied to the endpoint: Allowlisting is 'never trust' for execution; Ringfencing is 'assume breach' for approved apps; Elevation Control is 'least privilege' for admin rights; Network/Storage/Web extend the same thinking to every channel.",
"In interviews, anchor every product answer to the principle: 'Ringfencing exists because we assume the approved app is already compromised.' Principle-first answers sound like understanding; feature-first answers sound like memorization."],
"PRINCIPLE -> PRODUCT\n  never trust (execution)  -> Allowlisting\n  assume breach (behavior) -> Ringfencing\n  least privilege (admin)  -> Elevation Control\n  never trust (channels)   -> Network / Storage / Web",
"Practice the anchor: for each product, state the principle first, then the mechanism. 'Assume breach is why Word can't spawn PowerShell' \u2014 that sentence is the interview signal.",
["Treating Zero Trust as a product category \u2014 it's the principle; the products implement it.",
"Feature-first answers \u2014 anchor to the principle, then the mechanism.",
"Forgetting 'assume breach' \u2014 it's the sentence that explains Ringfencing."],
"For each of the six controls, write the principle-first sentence: '[principle] is why [control] [does X].'"),
L("zero-trust-philosophy", "The ninety-second prevention case",
["The case: detection classifies AFTER execution begins. Fileless techniques, living-off-the-land binaries, and zero-days execute before any verdict exists \u2014 the window every modern attack lives in. Prevention asks the question BEFORE execution: is this explicitly trusted?",
"The finite-vs-infinite frame: enumerating badness can never be complete (the infinite list); approving known-good is finite and knowable (the business's real footprint). Default-deny inverts the problem into the solvable shape.",
"The honest cost: operational weight \u2014 approvals, baselining, tuning. Name it unprompted: 'the tradeoff is real, and here's the machinery that pays it.' The candidate who names the cost is believed about the benefit.",
"The EDR respect: prevention shrinks what detection must chase; it doesn't replace it. 'EDR focuses on a smaller, higher-signal workload' \u2014 honor the panel's detection engineers while making the prevention case."],
"90-SECOND CASE\n  detection: AFTER execution (the window attacks live in)\n  prevention: BEFORE \u2014 'explicitly trusted?'\n  frame: finite known-good beats infinite known-bad\n  cost: real \u2014 Learning Mode, cert rules, approvals\n  EDR: respected \u2014 smaller, higher-signal workload",
"Rehearse the case until it's conversational: 90 seconds, no notes, the cost named unprompted, the EDR respect genuine. It's the most-asked philosophical question in the loop.",
["Skipping the cost \u2014 unprompted honesty is the credibility.",
"'Detection is obsolete' \u2014 never trash the panel's field.",
"Slogan version \u2014 the case needs the window, the frame, and the cost."],
"Deliver the case aloud twice: once at 90 seconds, once at 45. Note what survives the cut."),
L("zero-trust-philosophy", "Assume breach: designing for the inside",
["Assume breach means the walls already failed: the attacker has a foothold, a credential, a process. The design question shifts from 'keep them out' to 'limit what they can do in here.'",
"Endpoint translations: Ringfencing (the approved app is already compromised \u2014 leash it), no standing admin (the phished account must not inherit domain keys), egress allowlisting (the foothold must not phone home freely), storage control (the foothold must not walk out with data).",
"The interview sentence: 'We design as if Word is already compromised \u2014 that's why it can't spawn PowerShell.' One sentence that explains an entire product. Have three such sentences (Word, admin, egress).",
"The mindset test: when a scenario describes a trusted component misbehaving, the assume-breach candidate investigates the component \u2014 the trust-based candidate defends it. Interviewers watch which reflex you show."],
"ASSUME-BREACH SENTENCES\n  'We design as if Word is already compromised.'\n  'We design as if the account is already phished.'\n  'We design as if the agent is already beaconing.'",
"Memorize the three sentences. They're the assume-breach reflex in speakable form \u2014 and the panel listens for exactly this framing.",
["'Our walls are strong' \u2014 the pre-Zero-Trust sentence.",
"Defending the trusted component \u2014 investigate it instead.",
"One sentence only \u2014 prepare all three; scenarios vary."],
"Write your own fourth assume-breach sentence for Storage Control, then say all four aloud."),
L("zero-trust-philosophy", "Defending the philosophy under pushback",
["Pushback 1 \u2014 'Too strict, breaks usability': answer with the machinery (Learning Mode, Approval Center, certificate rules) and the staged rollout. Strictness is a deployment question, not a philosophy flaw \u2014 and usability is engineered, not wished.",
"Pushback 2 \u2014 'What about zero-days in approved apps?': answer with assume breach (Ringfencing) and honesty (no control covers everything; the human layer and practices complete it). The philosophy anticipates the question.",
"Pushback 3 \u2014 'We already have EDR': the respect pivot \u2014 EDR is vital, and prevention makes it better (smaller, higher-signal workload). Never position as replacement; position as the foundation.",
"The meta-skill: steelman the pushback before answering ('you're right that...') \u2014 then the principled response. Candidates who steelman sound like thinkers; candidates who deflect sound like marketers."],
"PUSHBACK PLAYBOOK\n  'too strict'      -> machinery + staged rollout (deployment, not flaw)\n  'zero-day in app' -> assume breach + honest limits\n  'we have EDR'     -> respect pivot (foundation, not replacement)\n  always: steelman first, then answer",
"Rehearse each pushback aloud: steelman (10 sec), principle (20 sec), machinery/limits (20 sec). The shape keeps you from rambling or deflecting.",
["Deflecting pushback \u2014 steelman it first, visibly.",
"Conceding the philosophy \u2014 the pushbacks attack deployment, not principle; keep them separated.",
"Rambling \u2014 the 50-second shape is the discipline."],
"Have a friend push back three times; answer each in the 50-second shape. Note where you ramble."),
]
