ch = {"id": "cyber-hero-culture", "name": "Cyber Hero Culture",
"description": "The service standard: 24/7/365 US-based MDR with ~60-second response \u2014 empathy plus competence, and demonstrating it in every answer.",
"icon": "\U0001f9b8", "scenarios": []}
ch_lessons = [
L("cyber-hero-culture", "The service standard",
["Cyber Hero is the 24/7/365 US-based MDR team with ~60-second average response: prevention technology plus human analysts who answer fast, triage well, and communicate plainly. The standard is the product as much as the software.",
"The standard's two halves: COMPETENCE (the triage is right, the fix is narrow, the validation is double) and EMPATHY (the customer feels heard, the timeline is clear, the language is plain). Either half alone is half the standard.",
"In interviews, every scenario answer is a Cyber Hero audition: does your answer lead with the human impact? Do you give a timeline before the technical detail? Do you close with what the customer should expect? The panel scores the service in the substance.",
"The anti-pattern: the brilliant technician who lectures the customer. Competence without empathy is a support ticket that escalates \u2014 and interviewers have all met that technician."],
"THE STANDARD\n  competence: right triage, narrow fix, double validation\n  empathy:    heard, timeline, plain language\n  every answer: impact first, then mechanism\n  anti-pattern: the brilliant lecturer",
"Audit your scenario answers: does each lead with impact, give a timeline, and close with expectations? If the empathy half is missing, the Cyber Hero answer is missing.",
["Leading with mechanism \u2014 impact first, always.",
"No timeline \u2014 'we're working on it' is not a timeline.",
"Lecturing the customer \u2014 plain language, never condescension."],
"Rewrite one scenario answer's opening to lead with the customer impact and a timeline."),
L("cyber-hero-culture", "De-escalation: the angry customer",
["The de-escalation sequence: ACKNOWLEDGE the impact first ('I hear you \u2014 payroll blocked is urgent'), then GATHER facts (app, version, onset), then TIMELINE ('here's what happens in the next 30 minutes'), then FIX narrowly, then FOLLOW UP (confirm resolved, explain the why).",
"Acknowledgment is not agreement: 'I hear you' doesn't concede the policy was wrong \u2014 it concedes the IMPACT is real. Customers de-escalate when the impact is acknowledged; they escalate when it's debated.",
"Never: blame the customer, blame the vendor, debate the policy's wisdom, or promise what you can't deliver. Each converts a solvable ticket into a relationship incident.",
"The follow-up is the loyalty builder: the customer who got the timeline, the fix, AND the plain-language why ('the update changed the app's fingerprint') becomes the advocate. Third-call-frustrated to loyal is the Cyber Hero story."],
"DE-ESCALATION\n  1. acknowledge impact ('I hear you \u2014 urgent')\n  2. gather facts (app, version, onset)\n  3. timeline (next 30 min, explicitly)\n  4. fix narrowly (scoped rule, validated)\n  5. follow up (confirm + explain the why)",
"Rehearse the sequence aloud with a frustrated 'customer' (friend, mirror). The acknowledgment-first reflex is the muscle \u2014 everything after it is technique.",
["Debating the impact \u2014 acknowledge, don't litigate.",
"Skipping the timeline \u2014 uncertainty escalates; timelines calm.",
"No follow-up \u2014 the fix without the why is a future repeat call."],
"Role-play the payroll-blocked call: 2 minutes, acknowledgment first, timeline by minute one."),
L("cyber-hero-culture", "Ownership: the mistake and the miss",
["Ownership is the culture's sharpest test: when YOU caused the block (bad rule, missed inventory), the Cyber Hero response is immediate acknowledgment, fast remediation, and the process fix \u2014 in that order, without defensiveness.",
"The ownership script: 'That was my rule change \u2014 I narrowed it too far. I'm rolling it back now (2 min), then I'll re-issue it correctly with a canary. Here's what I'm changing in my process so it doesn't repeat.' Mistake, fix, prevention \u2014 30 seconds.",
"Never: hide it, blame the tooling, minimize ('it was minor'), or fix it silently. Silent fixes teach the team that mistakes are shameful \u2014 and shameful mistakes get hidden, then repeated.",
"Interviewers probe ownership with 'tell me about a mistake': they're scoring the script, not the mistake. A good mistake story \u2014 owned, fixed, learned \u2014 outperforms a perfect record."],
"OWNERSHIP SCRIPT (30 sec)\n  'My change caused it \u2014 rolling back now (2 min).\n   Re-issuing correctly with a canary.\n   Process fix: [specific change] so it doesn't repeat.'",
"Prepare your mistake story in this script shape and rehearse it. The story's power is the process fix at the end \u2014 that's what interviewers remember.",
["Hiding or minimizing \u2014 the cover-up outranks the mistake.",
"Blaming tooling \u2014 ownership means the process, not the excuse.",
"No process fix \u2014 'I learned' without the change is sentiment."],
"Write your mistake story in the 30-second script shape. What's the process fix?"),
L("cyber-hero-culture", "Helpfulness as a competitive edge",
["Peer-voted 'most helpful' awards: the culture REWARDS helpfulness explicitly. In interviews, helpfulness shows as: answering the question behind the question, offering the path to yes, translating without being asked.",
"The helpful reflex in scenarios: after the verdict, add the enablement \u2014 'and here's how you'd avoid this next time,' 'the docs page for this is...,' 'want me to show your team the approval flow?' The fix solves today; the enablement prevents tomorrow.",
"Helpfulness scales: the free bootcamp, the conference, the community teaching \u2014 the company exports helpfulness. Candidates who teach (in answers, in the homelab write-up, in questions) signal the same instinct.",
"The test: 'explain it like I'm new.' The helpful candidate lights up; the insecure candidate condescends. Watch which one you are in the mock panel \u2014 and rehearse the generous version."],
"HELPFULNESS SIGNALS\n  answer the question behind the question\n  verdict + enablement ('avoid it next time: ...')\n  translate without being asked\n  teach generously (the bootcamp instinct)",
"Build one 'enablement' close for each of your STAR stories: what did the OTHER person learn? Helpfulness is remembered in the epilogue.",
["Verdict without enablement \u2014 the fix without the teaching.",
"Condescension disguised as teaching \u2014 generosity, not superiority.",
"Hoarding knowledge \u2014 the culture rewards the opposite."],
"Add an enablement close to your hardest triage story: what did the requester learn?"),
]
