# ---------------- technical-questions ----------------
tq = {"id": "technical-questions", "name": "Technical Questions",
"description": "The technical rounds: triage narration, rule-strength judgment, containment reasoning, and the hands-on depth interviewers probe.",
"icon": "\u2699\ufe0f", "scenarios": []}
tq_lessons = [
L("technical-questions", "Narrating triage: think out loud",
["Technical rounds are narrated thinking: the interviewer can't score silent analysis. Verbalize the loop \u2014 'I'm scoping first: which endpoints, which binary... now the audit: signed by the known vendor, hash changed...' \u2014 so every step is visible.",
"The narration has a pace: steady, not rushed. Pause to read (say 'let me look at the evidence'), then narrate the read. 'The evidence shows X, so my hypothesis is Y' \u2014 the sentence that turns observation into reasoning, aloud.",
"When stuck, narrate the stuckness: 'I'm torn between version-churn and push-regression \u2014 the timeline will decide: blocks started at 08:04, the push was at 08:00, so...' Showing the decision procedure earns credit even before the conclusion.",
"Close every technical answer with validation and communication: 'I'd validate the fix both ways and tell the user...' The close proves you operate, not just analyze."],
"NARRATION PACES\n  'Let me look at the evidence...' (pause, read)\n  'The evidence shows X, so my hypothesis is Y.'\n  'I'm torn between A and B \u2014 the [fact] decides.'\n  close: validation + communication, always",
"Practice narrating one scenario fully aloud, recorded. Listen back: is every inference spoken? The unspoken steps are unscored steps.",
["Silent analysis \u2014 unscored by definition.",
"Rushing the read \u2014 'let me look' is professional, not slow.",
"No close \u2014 analysis without the operational ending."],
"Record yourself narrating a triage end to end. Mark every silent inference \u2014 then speak them."),
L("technical-questions", "Rule-strength judgment on the spot",
["The interview probe: 'this app is blocked \u2014 how do you approve it?' The answer they want: the strength ladder applied \u2014 'Is it signed? Certificate rule, scoped. Unsigned but stable? Hash rule, narrow. Only a path? Then where \u2014 user-writable is a no.'",
"Say the ladder aloud: 'Certificate over hash over path \u2014 and I'd never do path in a user-writable location.' The ladder said plainly is the judgment signal; hemming is the uncertainty signal.",
"The follow-up is always scope: '...scoped to what?' Have the four axes ready: who, what, where, when. 'Finance group, this vendor's updater plus child, FIN workstations, permanent with quarterly review.'",
"The trap answers: global hash approval ('approve it everywhere'), path rules for convenience, and 'ask the user what they need' as the whole answer. Each is a scored miss \u2014 the panel has heard them all."],
"ON-THE-SPOT LADDER\n  signed?    -> certificate rule, scoped\n  unsigned?  -> hash rule, narrow + time-box\n  path only? -> where? user-writable = NO\n  always:    who/what/where/when (the scope)",
"Drill the ladder as a reflex: friend names an app state ('unsigned, updates monthly'), you answer the rule in one breath. Ten reps, no hesitation.",
["Global approvals \u2014 scope is the discipline.",
"Path-in-writable \u2014 the instant fail.",
"Hesitation on the ladder \u2014 drill it to reflex."],
"Ten rapid reps: app states in, rule-plus-scope out, one breath each."),
L("technical-questions", "Containment reasoning: the 'what now' questions",
["'Word spawned PowerShell and it was blocked \u2014 what now?' The answer: the block is a detection, not a resolution \u2014 isolate the host, identify the delivery (the document), hunt laterally for the delivery, verify via sibling leashes, fix the upstream path.",
"The reasoning order: CONTAIN (the behavior is already contained \u2014 verify the siblings), INVESTIGATE (delivery, scope, persistence), ERADICATE (remove the content, verify clean), LEARN (upstream fix). Say the order explicitly.",
"Distinguish the questions: 'may it run?' (allowlist) vs 'what may it do?' (ringfence). When the interviewer describes approved-app misbehavior, name which question it is BEFORE answering \u2014 that naming is the senior signal.",
"The wrong answers: 'remove Word from the allowlist' (breaks the business), 'close the ticket, control worked' (ignores the intrusion), 'reinstall Windows' (destroys evidence, skips the hunt)."],
"WHAT-NOW ORDER\n  contain:   verify siblings (already leashed?)\n  investigate: delivery, lateral scope, persistence\n  eradicate:  remove content, verify clean\n  learn:      upstream fix (filtering, macro policy)",
"Rehearse the 'what now' for three shapes: macro doc, malicious download, USB drop. Same order every time \u2014 the order is the reasoning.",
["'Control worked, closing' \u2014 the intrusion behind the block.",
"Allowlist answers to ringfence questions \u2014 name the question first.",
"Reinstall-first \u2014 evidence destroyed, hunt skipped."],
"Answer 'Word spawned PowerShell, blocked \u2014 what now?' aloud in the four-step order."),
L("technical-questions", "Depth probes: homelab and hands-on",
["'Tell me about your homelab' is the depth probe: they want specifics \u2014 what you built, what broke, what you configured, what you learned. Vague homelabs ('I have some VMs') fail; specific ones ('three-VM lab: DC, workstation, attacker; I practiced allowlist triage with...') pass.",
"Prepare three homelab stories: the BUILD (what and why), the BREAK (what failed and the debugging), the LESSON (what it taught about the real world). Each 60 seconds, technical specifics included.",
"'What would you do with no budget?' tests creativity under constraint: free tiers, open-source tooling, trial environments, community resources. The answer shows resourcefulness \u2014 a valued operator trait.",
"Honesty about depth: 'I haven't done X, but here's how I'd approach it' beats bluffing. Interviewers probe until you hit the edge \u2014 the edge handled honestly is the pass; the bluff is the fail."],
"HOMELAB STORIES (60s each)\n  build:  what, why (specifics)\n  break:  what failed, the debugging\n  lesson: what it taught (real-world link)\n  edge:   'haven't done X \u2014 here's my approach'",
"Write the three homelab stories with real specifics (names, configs, errors). If your homelab is thin, build one thing this week \u2014 even a single VM with a triage exercise \u2014 and story it.",
["Vague homelab \u2014 specifics or it didn't happen.",
"Bluffing past the edge \u2014 the probe continues until honesty.",
"No homelab at all \u2014 build one thing this week."],
"Write your build-break-lesson stories tonight. Time each at 60 seconds aloud."),
]
for _l in tq_lessons: new_lessons.append(_l)
