cu_lessons = [
L("customer-scenarios", "The customer call shape",
["Every customer scenario follows the shape: ACKNOWLEDGE (the impact is real), DIAGNOSE (evidence-first, narrated simply), TIMELINE (what happens next, explicitly), FIX (narrow, validated), CLOSE (confirm resolved, explain the why, leave a path).",
"The acknowledgment is the highest-leverage sentence: 'Payroll blocked the morning of payroll \u2014 I hear you, that's urgent, and I'm on it right now.' One sentence that de-escalates more than ten minutes of technical explanation.",
"Diagnose in plain language AS you go: 'I can see the block in our audit \u2014 your updater changed with its new version, and our system needs to learn the new one. That's a two-minute fix on my side.' The customer watches competence happen.",
"The close prevents the repeat call: confirm it's working, explain the why in one sentence, and give the direct path ('if it ever happens again, here's exactly what to send me'). Third calls become last calls."],
"CALL SHAPE\n  acknowledge: impact is real, you're on it (1 sentence)\n  diagnose:   evidence, narrated plainly (as you go)\n  timeline:   what happens next, explicitly\n  fix:        narrow, validated\n  close:      confirm + why + direct path",
"Rehearse the call shape with a timer: acknowledgment in the first 15 seconds, timeline by minute one. The shape is the service skill \u2014 interviewers score its presence, not just the fix.",
["Technical monologue \u2014 narrate plainly AS you diagnose.",
"No timeline \u2014 uncertainty escalates; timelines calm.",
"Close without the why \u2014 the repeat call is born here."],
"Role-play a blocked-payroll call in the five-step shape, 3 minutes, acknowledgment first."),
L("customer-scenarios", "The confused user",
["Confused users aren't wrong users: 'my computer says no' is a valid bug report from their side of the glass. Your job: translate their confusion into diagnostic data with kind, specific questions.",
"The question technique: one question at a time, plain words, no jargon \u2014 'What were you trying to open when it said no? Can you read me what the message says, word for word?' Each answer is a diagnostic step; rushing the questions loses the data.",
"Never make them feel foolish: 'That's a really common one \u2014 here's what's happening...' The user's dignity is part of the fix. Condescension is the service failure that no technical brilliance repairs.",
"Teach in the close: one sentence on what happened and one on what to do next time. The confused user who understands becomes the confident user \u2014 and the advocate."],
"KIND DIAGNOSTICS\n  one question at a time, plain words\n  'read me the message, word for word'\n  'that's a common one \u2014 here's what's happening'\n  close: one sentence of teaching",
"Practice the kind-diagnostic questions aloud: they're harder than they look \u2014 every jargon word you catch yourself using is a point lost. Record and count them.",
["Jargon questions \u2014 'what's the hash?' to a confused user.",
"Rushing \u2014 the data comes at their pace, not yours.",
"Impatience leaking \u2014 they hear it before you feel it."],
"Write five kind-diagnostic questions for 'my app won't open.' Zero jargon allowed."),
L("customer-scenarios", "The MSP technician",
["The MSP technician is a peer under pressure: they have THEIR client breathing down their neck, and you're their escalation path. Treat them as a colleague, not a customer \u2014 technical depth welcome, peer respect required.",
"Give them relayable status: everything you say should be quotable to their client. 'Contained at 14:22, root cause identified, fix validating now, update in 30 minutes' \u2014 they forward it verbatim, and you just made them look good.",
"Share the diagnostic reasoning, not just the verdict: MSP techs learn from your triage, and the next ticket from them arrives better-formed. Teaching the channel compounds.",
"Respect their context: they manage dozens of clients; your issue is one of fifty today. Brevity plus completeness \u2014 the peer standard."],
"MSP PEER STANDARD\n  colleague, not customer (depth welcome)\n  relayable status (quotable to THEIR client)\n  share the reasoning (they learn; tickets improve)\n  brevity + completeness (their day has 50 fires)",
"Rehearse one MSP call: give the status update you'd want forwarded to an angry client. If it wouldn't calm the client, rewrite it.",
["Customer-voice to a peer \u2014 condescension to a technician.",
"Unrelayable status \u2014 'we're looking into it' helps no one.",
"Verdict without reasoning \u2014 the teaching moment wasted."],
"Write the relayable status for: contained breach, 4 hosts, investigating. Three sentences."),
L("customer-scenarios", "The executive escalation",
["Executive escalations are business events wearing technical clothes: the VP doesn't care about the hash \u2014 they care about the payroll run, the deadline, the board meeting. Lead with the business impact and the timeline; the mechanism is appendix material.",
"The executive shape: IMPACT acknowledged ('I understand payroll is down'), TIMELINE given ('fix validating now, resolved in 20 minutes'), BUSINESS framing ('no data at risk; this was a policy update, not an attack'), and the FOLLOW-UP committed ('I'll send the postmortem summary by EOD').",
"Never: technical deep-dives unprompted, blame (theirs or yours), or 'it's complicated.' Executives buy confidence and clarity \u2014 the mechanism is available on request, not by default.",
"The follow-up is the relationship: the EOD summary (what happened, why, what prevents recurrence \u2014 one page) turns the escalation into trust. Executives remember the follow-through, not the fire."],
"EXECUTIVE SHAPE\n  impact:    acknowledged in business terms\n  timeline:  explicit, short\n  framing:   'policy update, not an attack' (risk stated)\n  follow-up: one-page summary by EOD, committed",
"Draft the EOD summary for a payroll-block incident: what happened, why, recurrence prevention \u2014 one page, zero jargon.",
["Mechanism-first to executives \u2014 business terms first, always.",
"'It's complicated' \u2014 the confidence killer.",
"No follow-up committed \u2014 the escalation without the close."],
"Deliver the executive update for the payroll block: 60 seconds, business terms, timeline first."),
]
